   "answer": "Based on the lecture evidence, here is the explanation:\n\n**Which data should be migrated to HBM?**\nData that is **hot, latency-critical, and frequently reused** should be kept in or migrated to HBM. Conversely, large, cold objects should be placed in the LPDDR capacity tier [S1, p. 69].\n\n**When is migration worthwhile?**\nMigration (specifically promotion from the capacity tier to HBM) is worthwhile only when the **future reuse of the data saves more than the transfer and bookkeeping cost** associated with moving it [S1, p. 69].\n\n**Reasoning:**\nThe lecture describes a tiered memory management strategy where HBM is reserved for high-performance needs (low latency, high reuse) while LPDDR provides capacity for less frequently accessed data. The decision to move data between these tiers is cost-benefit driven: the performance gain from faster access in HBM must outweigh the overhead of the data transfer and management overhead.\n\n**Citations:**\n*   [S1] Page 69: \"Keep hot, latency-critical, frequently reused data in HBM. Place large, cold objects in the LPDDR capacity tier.\"\n*   [S1] Page 69: \"Promotion helps only when future reuse saves more than the transfer and bookkeeping cost.\"",
